// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
// kernel/sys.fi -- round K4: the system call table of Osum.
// This round lays the POSIX floor. Three rules, and they are the whole
// design:
//
//   1. THE NUMBERS ARE LINUX'S NUMBERS. `read` is 0, `write` is 1, `open`
//      is 2, `exit` is 60, `fork` is 57 -- the x86-64 table of
//      `arch/x86/entry/syscalls/syscall_64.tbl`. Not out of reverence:
//      stage 2 of this plan is to run a statically linked Linux binary,
//      and such a binary puts its number in rax and its fourth argument in
//      r10 without asking anybody. Every number we pick differently is a
//      translation table somebody has to write and keep right. Where Osum
//      has something Linux does not, the number is >= 1000 -- far above
//      anything Linux will ever hand out -- and `docs/ROUNDK4.md` lists
//      every one of them.
//   2. NOTHING IS ANSWERED SILENTLY. An unknown number gives -ENOSYS, a
//      pointer that is not the caller's gives -EFAULT (checked BEFORE it
//      is followed, `proc.user_ok`), a bad descriptor gives -EBADF. Errors
//      are NEGATIVE, out of the one list in `kernel/errno.fi`.
//   3. THE KERNEL NEVER HOLDS A POINTER OF RING 3. Names, argument
//      vectors and structures are copied into the data area first, and
//      what goes back is copied out page by page with a check per page.
//
// THE TWO MARKS OF ROUND 59 (numbers 1 and 2) collide with `write` and
// `open`, and they lose -- with one exception. `user.run` sets
// `kstate.EXCURSION` while the hand written program of `isr.s` is in ring
// 3, and only while that flag stands do 1 and 2 mean the marks. That
// program is a mode of the kernel, not a process; nothing else can reach
// those numbers, and section 3 of `tools/kernel/run.sh` goes on measuring
// exactly what it measured in round 59.

